{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-c8e49056-9b7a-4711-805e-8b442e33c312","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f765c6d745f0342","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8007-ozb06qk9/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8007-E006.md","content":"# Dashboard RBAC extension and feature-flag manifests for Feature Store admin CRUD\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the odh-dashboard's Kubernetes RBAC and deploy-time configuration so platform administrators can create, update, patch, and delete FeatureStore custom resources through the dashboard, and so the new admin UI can be rolled out progressively behind a feature flag. This is a manifest-and-configuration change — no new backend routes or BFF modules.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Extend the dashboard ClusterRole (`manifests/core-bases/base/sa-rbac/cluster-role.yaml`) with `create, update, patch, delete` verbs for `feast.dev/featurestores`, preserving existing `get, list, watch`. Use the feast-operator's editor ClusterRole as the reference for the minimum permission set; reference the role variant actually bound in target namespaces.\n- Add the Feature Store admin UI feature flag default to OdhDashboardConfig for progressive rollout.\n- Verify that platform administrators hold `feast.dev/featurestores` write verbs in target namespaces once the extension is applied (a needs-validation assumption in the strategy).\n- Confirm the two-tier authorization model: admin writes enforced at the K8s API level via the user's forwarded token through kube-rbac-proxy, plus the dashboard's admin-route middleware check; viewer users unchanged (read-only via the existing viewer ClusterRole).\n- Preserve security posture: TLS 1.2+ via service-ca, no new external network connections, FIPS compliance unchanged (manifest-only changes, no new Go BFF module).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The dashboard ClusterRole grants `create, update, patch, delete` (in addition to existing `get, list, watch`) for `feast.dev/featurestores`.\n- Admin users can create and delete FeatureStore CRs through the dashboard's K8s API pass-through; non-admin users without the editor role binding cannot write FeatureStore CRs regardless of UI state (enforcement is at the K8s API, not only UI hiding).\n- Viewer permissions are unchanged: `get, list, watch` on `featurestores` and `featurestores/status` only.\n- The feature flag default lands per the rollout plan; clusters without the Feast component enabled see no dashboard changes.\n- No new external network connections; existing TLS and FIPS posture is preserved.\n- Rollback: reverting the manifest restores the prior RBAC state with no data migration (backwards compatible — no changes to the Feast CRD schema or operator behavior).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables the P0 HLRs for deploying (create) and deleting FeatureStore instances from the dashboard.\n- Covers the enforcement side of the P1 viewer read-only HLR (K8s RBAC tier).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":46112,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b79643b640be188f","agent_id":"af2e9536495148b77","session_id":"420e9024-f833-42df-9d31-a5dd457541d8","uuid":"44ce1b84-091b-4e44-bd8b-f102399985dd","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:22:16.676Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8007"}
