{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ace39217ffe8aa6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8007\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n9\t  description: 'RHAISTRAT-8007-E003 lists Investigation RHAISTRAT-8007-E001 in dependencies\n10\t    (the summary labels the E001 to E003 edge a True gate: rewrite of design scope)\n11\t    but has gated_by: null and no gate_failure_impact. Set gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8007-E001\n12\t    with gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite and a fallback_approach describing redesigning\n13\t    the wireframes to the validated TP field set, so automated pipeline consumers\n14\t    can detect the gate.'\n15\t- severity: major\n16\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n17\t  description: 'RHAISTRAT-8007-E004 lists Investigations RHAISTRAT-8007-E001 and RHAISTRAT-8007-E002\n18\t    in dependencies, but gated_by names only E001 — the E002 prototype-alignment gate\n19\t    (summary labels the E002 to E004 edge a True gate: rewrite) is unrecorded in machine-readable\n20\t    metadata even though the E004 gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach text covers\n21\t    it. Record the E002 gate explicitly in the gating metadata with its own rewrite\n22\t    fallback so each Investigation gate is machine-detectable.'\n23\terror: null\n24\t---\n25\t## Review Summary\n26\t\n27\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n28\tRecommendation: accept\n29\t\n30\tFull decomposition (triage: null, epic_count 8 — no triage consistency issue; multi-epic output is expected). The DAG is internally consistent, HLR coverage is complete, and strategy context is carried forward thoroughly. One criterion lost a point for incomplete gating metadata on two Investigation dependencies.\n31\t\n32\t## Criterion Details\n33\t\n34\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n35\t\n36\tAll 8 HLRs from the strategy are mapped in the traceability matrix and verified against epic HLR Traceability sections: the five P0 HLRs (create wizard, list, inspect, delete, real-time status) map to E004/E005; the P1 viewer read-only HLR maps to E005 (UI gating), E006 (K8s RBAC enforcement), and E008 (validation); the P1 air-gapped HLR maps to E004 (static bundling) and E008 (air-gapped cluster validation); the P2 inline-validation HLR is covered by E004's scope and ACs. The P2 bundling into E004 is justified, not priority collapse: inline validation shares the wizard's form surface and validation-rule model and is not independently deferrable — the decomposition documents this explicitly (Health Warning 1). Priority inheritance is correct: E001/E002/E003 (P0) gate P0 implementation work; E006 (P0) enables P0 create/delete; E007 is docs-authoring and exempt (strategy priority Major → P1); E008 is terminal (nothing depends on it), so P1 is not an inheritance violation.\n37\t\n38\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n39\t\n40\tAll 12 diagram edges match the target epics' frontmatter `dependencies` exactly, and vice versa (E001/E002/E006 have no dependencies, matching the diagram's lack of incoming edges). Arrows are drawn dependency → dependent per convention. No cycles. No invalid edges — every epic referenced exists as a main-plan epic; no branch files exist and none are referenced. Each edge is justified under the construction rules: Investigation gates (E001/E002 → downstream) per Rule 3, artifact producer → consumer (E003 → E004/E005) per Rule 12, docs-last (E004/E005/E006 → E007) per Rule 11, and consumer-of-built-artifact (E004/E005/E006 → E008) per Rule 12. The E006 ∥ E004/E005 parallelism is handled correctly: the ClusterRole extension is a configuration reference coupled via ACs on E004/E005 (\"succeed at runtime once the expanded ClusterRole is applied\"), not a DAG edge — exactly the required treatment. E004 ∥ E005 are distinct UI surfaces sharing only existing proxies, correctly parallel. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 cannot be violated. Critical path E001 → E003 → E004 → E007/E008 (length 4) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4). No data/artifact dependency is discoverable in epic content that is missing from the DAG: E004 consumes E001's field inventory and E002's reuse plan (both edges present); E005 consumes E002's assessment (edge present) and E003's wireframes (edge present).\n41\t\n42\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n43\t\n44\tEach epic has a single component/team tuple: E001 (Feature Store / Feature Store Engineering), E002/E004/E005/E006 (AI Core Dashboard / Dashboard-Frontend Engineering), E003 (UXD / UXD Team), E007 (Documentation / Documentation Team), E008 (QE / QE Team). No epic bundles multiple teams. The three same-team dashboard epics are split along a clean surface axis (create wizard vs management views vs RBAC/flag manifests) for scoped review cycles — a valid split, not duplication: E004 builds the create form, E005 builds list/detail/delete/status against the same CR data via existing proxies, E006 owns only manifests. The feature-flag mention in E004/E005 (UI-side gating code) vs E006 (flag default manifest) is incidental config overlap, not scope duplication. No work-product type mixing: code (E004/E005/E006), design authoring (E003), docs (E007), and validation (E008) are separate epics. No epic is clearly oversized — E005 bundles list/detail/delete/status, but these form one cohesive management surface and fit within a single sprint.\n45\t\n46\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n47\t\n48\tTypes are correct: E001 and E002 are genuine Investigations whose outcomes (TP field set + validated CRD constraints; prototype reuse determination) change downstream scope/approach, and both have downstream dependents; all Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts; E003 (wireframes) and E007 (docs-authoring) are correctly typed Implementation. However, gating metadata is incomplete on two edges the decomposition summary itself labels \"True gate